WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.360
Walking into a sequel as hotly anticipated
as Deadpool 2 is scary, since the very notion

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.780
of a flick that follows up the hyper-successful
original Deadpool raises weighty questions:

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.200
Will it be as funny, raunchy, and action-packed?

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.240
Can Ryan Reynolds really strike gold twice
as the Merc with a Mouth?

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:21.819
And if we just saw Josh Brolin as Thanos in
Avengers: Infinity War, can we actually enjoy

00:00:21.819 --> 00:00:22.939
him as Cable?

00:00:22.939 --> 00:00:25.470
"Your time's up, you dumb f---."

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.869
Well, that's just lazy writing."

00:00:27.869 --> 00:00:31.839
Take a deep breath, Deadpool fans: we now
have answers to your questions.

00:00:31.839 --> 00:00:36.110
Ahead of its May 18th release, Deadpool 2
premiered early screenings to critics, who

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:40.480
have wasted no time in revealing their thoughts
about the film that also stars Zazie Beetz

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:46.550
as Domino, Terry Crews as Bedlam, Lewis Tan
as Shatterstar, Morena Baccarin as Vanessa,

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:53.050
Julian Dennison as Russell, Stefan Kapičić
as Colossus, Leslie Uggams as Blind Al, Karan

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:58.500
Soni as Dopinder, Bill Skarsgård as Zeitgeist,
and Rob Delaney as Peter.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:03.420
And first reactions are promising, proving
that yes, sequels can be as good, or better,

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:04.760
than the original.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:08.810
ScreenRant news editor Molly Freeman said
she enjoyed the film more than its predecessor,

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:09.810
tweeting,

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:11.960
"So I liked Deadpool 2 more than the first
movie.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.130
I think it’s more ambitious, and it pays
off.

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:18.189
There’s plenty of humor, action &amp; drama,
and the movie balances it well.

00:01:18.189 --> 00:01:22.299
Cable &amp; Domino are standouts (I love them
and I can’t wait for X-Force).

00:01:22.299 --> 00:01:24.549
Plus there are some FUN surprises."

00:01:24.549 --> 00:01:28.329
However, Freeman mentioned that the sequel,
quote, "makes a narrative decision that is

00:01:28.329 --> 00:01:29.329
not great."

00:01:29.329 --> 00:01:32.219
Of course she didn't spoil it by mentioning
details.

00:01:32.219 --> 00:01:35.460
Might she be talking about scandal-embroiled
actor T.J.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:37.929
Miller, who portrays Weasel in Deadpool 2?

00:01:37.929 --> 00:01:42.470
Matt Singer of ScreenCrush had a similar experience
watching the movie, since he "wasn't a huge

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:43.470
fan" of the original film.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:44.470
He tweeted,

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:48.649
"Slow to start but all the stuff with X-Force
and Cable (and Peter!) works surprisingly

00:01:48.649 --> 00:01:49.649
well."

00:01:49.649 --> 00:01:53.889
Another critic who really disliked the OG
Deadpool but was impressed by the sequel was

00:01:53.889 --> 00:01:56.070
Uproxx's Mike Ryan, who tweeted:

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:59.920
"Deadpool 2 beat me into submission until
I was actually enjoying myself.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.359
Also it features my now favorite post-credit
scene.

00:02:02.359 --> 00:02:05.670
I'm still legit shocked that I enjoyed this
movie."

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:10.470
IndieWire's Kate Erbland also mentioned the
mysterious after-movie scene, tweeting,

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:14.190
"Deadpool 2: I'll say this, it earns the hell
out of that R rating.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:15.190
And the cameos.

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:16.450
And the post-credit scenes."

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:20.530
Indie filmmaker Sammy Paul says fans will
want to stay after the credits, and praised

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:24.820
the sequel for managing to be both "subversive
and weirdly sweet" while still having "all

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:27.340
the hilarity and self-awareness of the first
film."

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:31.300
Collider editor-in-chief, Steven Weintraub,
mirrored that sentiment, writing,

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:36.040
"Happy to report Deadpool 2 is a lot of fun
and had me laughing beginning to end.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:37.980
Stuff after the credits is awesome.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:40.560
All the people added to the film were perfectly
cast."

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.440
CinemaBlend managing director Sean O'Connell
called Deadpool 2 "the perfect sequel", tweeting

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:46.440
that it

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:49.570
"doubles down on everything that fans love
about the original, and fixes some of that

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:50.570
film's issues.

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:53.910
Huge, huge laughs that play the audience like
a fiddle.

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:56.210
[Ryan Reynolds] destroys as Wade.

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:58.430
Great action, fantastic comic nods.

00:02:58.430 --> 00:02:59.560
A killer ride!"

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:04.480
But arguably the best and boldest reaction
to Deadpool 2 came from Vox senior reporter

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:10.320
Alex Abad-Santos, who definitively stated:
"Deadpool 2 is better than Infinity War."

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:13.500
Find out for yourself when Deadpool 2 hits
theaters on May 18th.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.520
"It lives up to the hype, plus, plus…

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:17.520
Stop at 2.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:18.520
You killed it."

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:19.520
Thanks for watching!

